# The client's routes, answered at the host root as well as under /app/.
#
# Not a legacy mapping: this origin has never served anything, so no link at
# these paths has ever been minted. It is here because the split between a
# static root and a client under /app/ is ours rather than the user's, and a
# person who types or bookmarks the route they see in the app should land on
# it. The cost of being wrong in the other direction is a 404 on an invite.
#
# 301 and not 302: the split is not a temporary arrangement, and a permanent
# redirect is what lets a browser stop asking and a crawler stop listing the
# bare path as a separate page.
#
# Like _headers, this file is parsed rather than served, must sit at the root of
# the assets directory, and does not apply to anything the Worker answers
# itself. That last point is why /app/* is deliberately absent: a deep link
# matching no asset is answered with the client's own document, which is a
# rewrite and not a redirect, because the URL the person opened has to survive
# into go_router rather than be replaced in their address bar.

/g/*       /app/g/:splat     301
/join/*    /app/join/:splat  301
/welcome   /app/welcome      301
/archived  /app/archived     301
/account   /app/account      301
/settings  /app/settings     301
